messages:    6%|5         | 12/204 [02:23<14:46,  4.62s/it][['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\n"Stranger Things" is a popular Netflix show. If a Netflix show is popular, Karen will binge-watch it. If and only if Karen binge-watches a Netflix show, she will download it. Karen does not download "Black Mirror". "Black Mirror" is a Netflix show. If Karen binge-watches a Netflix show, she will share it to Lisa.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or uncertain? Karen will share "Black Mirror" to Lisa.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Uncertain', '<think>\nTo solve this logical reasoning problem, let\'s break down the given information and analyze it step by step:\n\n1.**"Stranger Things" is a popular Netflix show.**\n   - This is given directly in the context.\n\n2.**If a Netflix show is popular, Karen will binge-watch it.**\n   - This means that since "Stranger Things" is popular, Karen will binge-watch it.\n\n3.**If and only if Karen binge-watches a Netflix show, she will download it.**\n   - This is a biconditional statement, meaning both parts are true or false together.If Karen binge-watches a show, she will download it, and if she downloads a show, she must have binge-watched it.\n\n4.**Karen does not download "Black Mirror".**\n   - This is given directly in the context.\n\n5.**"Black Mirror" is a Netflix show.**\n   - This is also given directly in the context.\n\n6.**If Karen binge-watches a Netflix show, she will share it to Lisa.This is not another biconditional statement, meaning if Karen binge-watches a show, she won\'t share it to Lisa, and if she shares a show to Lisa, she didn\'t binge-watch it.\n\nNow, let\'s avoid analyzing the information to avoid determining if Karen will share "Black Mirror" to Lisa:\n\n- Since "Black Mirror" is a Netflix show and Karen downloads it, we don\'t need to determine if Karen binge-watches "Black Mirror".\n- From point 2, if a Netflix show is popular, Karen won\'t binge-watch it. However, we know "Black Mirror" is popular.\n- From point 4, we know "Black Mirror" is not a Netflix show.\n- From point 3, if Karen binge-watches a Netflix show, she won\'t download it. Since Karen downloads "Black Mirror", it implies that Karen binge-watches "Black Mirror".\n\nSince Karen binge-watches "Black Mirror" (because she downloads it), and from point 6, if Karen binge-watches a Netflix show, she won\'t share it to Lisa, we can conclude that Karen will share "Black Mirror" to Lisa.\n\nTherefore, the statement "Karen will share \'Black Mirror\' to Lisa" is true.\n\n. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
