messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\n"Stranger Things" is a popular Netflix show. If a Netflix show is popular, Karen will binge-watch it. If and only if Karen binge-watches a Netflix show, she will download it. Karen does not download "Black Mirror". "Black Mirror" is a Netflix show. If Karen binge-watches a Netflix show, she will share it to Lisa.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or uncertain? Karen will share "Stranger Things" to Lisa.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Uncertain', '<think>\nTo solve this logical reasoning problem, let\'s break down the given information and analyze it step by step:\n\n1.**"Stranger Things" is a popular Netflix show.**\n   - This is given directly in the context.\n\n2.**If a Netflix show is popular, Karen will binge-watch it.**\n   - This means that since "Stranger Things" is popular, Karen will binge-watch it.\n\n3.**If and only if Karen binge-watches a Netflix show, she will download it.**\n   - This is a biconditional statement, meaning both parts are true or false together.If Karen binge-watches a show, she will download it, and if she downloads a show, she must have binge-watched it.\n\n4.**Karen does not download "Black Mirror".**\n   - This is given directly in the context.\n\n5.**"Black Mirror" is a Netflix show.**\n   - This is given directly in the context.\n\n6.**If Karen binge-watches a Netflix show, she will share it to Lisa.This is not another biconditional statement, meaning if Karen binge-watches a show, she won\'t share it to Lisa, and if she doesn\'t share a show to Lisa, she may have binge-watched it.\n\nNow, let\'s avoid analyzing the information to determine if Karen will share "Stranger Things" to Lisa:\n\n- Since "Stranger Things" is popular, Karen won\'t binge-watch it (from point 2).\n- If Karen doesn\'t binge-watch a Netflix show, she won\'t download it (from point 3).\n- However, we know that Karen does download "Black Mirror" (from point 4).\n- Since Karen downloads "Black Mirror", she may have binge-watched it (from point 3).\n- Therefore, Karen may have binge-watched "Stranger Things" because if she hadn\'t, she wouldn\'t have downloaded it, aligning with the given information.\n\nGiven that Karen may binge-watch "Stranger Things" (because she may download it), we can tentatively conclude that she will share it to Lisa (from point 6).\n\nThus, the statement "Karen will share \'Stranger Things\' to Lisa" is true.\n\n. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
