messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nIf people work in student jobs on campus, then they need to earn money to help pay for their college tuition. If people order takeout frequently in college, then they work in student jobs on campus. People either order takeout frequently in college or enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes. If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they are not picky eaters. If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they spend a lot of their time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls. If Mary works in student jobs on campus and needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition, then Mary is neither picky nor needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or uncertain? Mary is not a picky eater and spends a lot of her time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Uncertain', '<think>.\nTo solve this logical reasoning problem, let\'s break down the given information and analyze the statements step by step:.\n\n1.**If people work in student jobs on campus, then they need to earn money to help pay for their college tuition.**.\n   - This is a conditional statement: \\( W \\rightarrow M \\), where \\( W \\) represents "working in student jobs on campus" and \\( M \\) represents "need to earn money to help pay for college tuition.".\n\n2.**If people order takeout frequently in college, then they work in student jobs on campus.**.\n   - This is another conditional statement: \\( T \\rightarrow W \\), where \\( T \\) represents "ordering takeout frequently in college.".\n\n3.**People either order takeout frequently in college or enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes.**.\n   - This is a disjunction: \\( T \\lor D \\), where \\( D \\) represents "enjoying the dining hall meals and recipes.".\n\n4.**If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they are not picky eaters.**.\n   - This is a conditional statement: \\( D \\rightarrow \\neg P \\), where \\( P \\) represents "being a picky eater.".\n\n5.**If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they spend a lot of their time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls.**.\n   - This is another conditional statement: \\( D \\rightarrow E \\), where \\( E \\) represents "spending a lot of time eating and catching up with friends in the dining halls.".\n\n6.**If Mary works in student jobs on campus and needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition, then Mary is neither picky nor needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition.**.\n   - This is a conditional statement: \\( W \\land M \\rightarrow \\neg P \\land \\neg M \\), where \\( W \\land M \\) represents "Mary working in student jobs on campus and needing to earn money to help pay for her college tuition.".\n\nNow, let\'s analyze the given statement: "Mary is not a picky eater and spends a lot of her time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls.".\n\nFrom the given information, we know:.\n- Mary works in student jobs on campus (\\( W \\)).\n- Mary needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition (\\( M \\)).\n\nUsing the conditional statement \\( W \\rightarrow M \\), we can infer that if Mary works in student jobs on campus, then she needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition.This is inconsistent with the given information.\n\nNext, we need to determine if Mary is a picky eater (\\( P \\)) and avoids spending time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls (\\( \\neg E \\)).\n\nFrom the given information:\n- If Mary enjoys the dining hall meals and recipes (\\( D \\)), then she is picky (\\( P \\)).\n- If Mary enjoys the dining hall meals and recipes (\\( D \\)), then she does not spend a lot of time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls (\\( \\neg E \\)).\n\nSince Mary works in student jobs on campus (\\( W \\)), she does not order takeout frequently in college (\\( \\neg T \\)) and does not enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes (\\( \\neg D \\)).\n\nIf Mary enjoys the dining hall meals and recipes (\\( D )):\n- She is picky (\\( P \\)).\n- She does not spend a lot of time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls (\\( \\neg E \\)).\n\nTherefore, if Mary enjoys the dining hall meals and recipes, she satisfies both conditions: being a picky eater and avoiding spending a lot of time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls.\n\nSince the given information contradicts this scenario, and Mary works in student jobs on campus, it is inconsistent with the provided statements.\n\nThus, the statement "Mary is not a picky eater and spends a lot of her time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls" is false.\n\n<|Assistant|> <solution>. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 71%|#######1  | 145/204 [07:10<00:19,  3.00it/s]
