ie's Statement: \"Bob is a Type C person.\"**\n   - If Charlie is a Type A person, he always tells the truth, so Bob must be a Type C person.\n   - If Charlie is a Type B person, he always lies, so Bob must be a Type A person.\n   - If Charlie is a Type C person, he sometimes tells the truth and sometimes lies, so Bob could be either a Type A or a Type C person.\n\nNow, let's analyze the possible combinations:\n\n**Case 1: Alice is a Type A person.**\n- Alice tells the truth: Bob is a Type A person.\n- Bob's statement: Alice is a Type B person (lies, so Bob is not a Type A person).\n- This is a contradiction, so Alice cannot be a Type A person.\n\n**Case 2: Alice is a Type B person.**\n- Alice lies: Bob is a Type C person.\n- Bob's statement: Alice is a Type B person (true, so Alice is a Type B person).\n- This is consistent, so Alice can be a Type B person.\n- Bob is a Type C person.\n- Charlie's statement: Bob is a Type C person (true, so Charlie is a Type A person).\n- This is consistent, so Charlie can be a Type A person.\n\n**Case 3: Alice is a Type C person.**\n- Alice sometimes tells the truth and sometimes lies.\n  - If Alice tells the truth: Bob is a Type A person.\n  - If Alice lies: Bob is a Type C person.\n- Bob's statement: Alice is a Type B person (false, so Bob is a Type C person).\n- This is consistent, so Alice can be a Type C person.\n- Bob is a Type C person.\n- Charlie's statement: Bob is a Type C person (true, so Charlie is a Type A person).\n- This is consistent, so Charlie can be a Type A person.\n\nFrom the analysis, the consistent scenario is:\n- Alice is a Type C person.\n- Bob is a Type C person.\n- Charlie is a Type A person.\n\nTherefore, the correct option is:\n\n<answer>\nC\n</answer>\n</think>",
